        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nCan I just set a field on currentState() inside my KV entity handler and return done?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-key-value-entities/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "3e304803a50c8734927e8af6990274b49b4247cea2e2e209cea68d65819ead49",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No. Changes you make to `currentState()` aren't persisted. State is only written when you return an effect that says so:\n\n```java\npublic Effect<Done> setName(String name) {\n  var updated = currentState().withName(name);   // new immutable copy\n  return effects().updateState(updated).thenReply(Done.getInstance());\n}\n```\n\nReturning `effects().reply(Done.getInstance())` with a mutated object does nothing durable. The entity stores no state, and the next call sees the old value (or the in-memory object, until the entity passivates, which hides the bug).\n\nUse records or other immutable types for state so mutation isn't possible in the first place. Before the first write, `currentState()` returns what `emptyState()` gives you, which is `null` by default. Override `emptyState()` if you want a usable default."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
        "testCase": {
          "description": "mutate",
          "vars": {
